Here is the implementation of the Bisection Method in Java. This method is used to find roots of an equation.

```java
public class BisectionMethod {
    private static final double ACCURACY = 0.01;

    // Function that takes a double and returns a boolean indicating if it's the root of the function
    interface RootChecker {
        boolean isRoot(double x);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(bisectionMethod(-10, 10, BisectionMethod::f));
    }

    // Function to find the root of
    private static double f(double x) {
        return x * x - 2; // This function has a root at sqrt(2)
    }

    public static double bisectionMethod(double a, double b, RootChecker checker) {
        if (f(a) * f(b) >= 0) {
            System.out.println("Invalid interval");
            return Double.NaN;
        }

        double c = a;
        while ((b - a) >= ACCURACY) {
            c = (a + b) / 2;

            if (checker.isRoot(c)) return c;

            else if (f(c) * f(a) < 0) b = c;

            else a = c;
        }

        return c;
    }
}
```
This program finds the root of the function `x^2 - 2` using the bisection method. The bisection method is a root-finding algorithm that repeatedly bisects an interval and then selects a subinterval in which a root exists for further refinement. It is most useful when the function is continuous within the interval, and there is at least one root in the interval.

The `RootChecker` interface is used to check if a given number is the root of the function. In this case, it's simply checking if the value of the function at that point is close enough to zero (within the defined accuracy). If it is, then we can consider that number as the root of the function.

