Here is the implementation of the Bisection Method in Java. This method is used to find roots of an equation.

```java
public class BisectionMethod {
    private static final double ACCURACY = 0.01; // define accuracy level

    // function that takes a double as input and returns a double
    static double f(double x) {
        return (x * x * x) - (2 * x) - 5;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double a = -100.0, b = 100.0; // define initial interval [a,b]

        if (f(a) * f(b) >= 0) {
            System.out.println("Bisection method fails.");
            return;
        }

        double c = a;
        while ((b - a) >= ACCURACY) {
            // find mid point
            c = (a + b) / 2;

            if (f(c) == 0.0) {
                break; // if root is found, break the loop
            } else if (f(c) * f(a) < 0) {
                b = c; // update interval [a, b] to [a, c]
            } else {
                a = c; // update interval [a, b] to [c, b]
            }
        }

        System.out.printf("The root is: %.4f\n", c);
    }
}
```
This program defines a function `f` that takes a double as input and returns the value of the equation `x^3 - 2x -5` at this point. The main method initializes an interval [a,b] and checks if it contains a root using the sign change condition. If not, it prints an error message and terminates.

The bisection algorithm is then run until the difference between b and a is less than ACCURACY. In each iteration, it calculates the mid point c of [a,b], checks if f(c) equals 0 (root found) or if the sign of f(c) changes with respect to f(a), updates the interval accordingly.

Finally, it prints the root calculated by bisection method upto 4 decimal places.

