Here is a simple implementation of a stack in Java using an array. This class has methods to push, pop and peek elements from the stack:

```java
public class Stack {
    private int maxSize;
    private Object[] stackArray;
    private int top;

    // constructor
    public Stack(int size) {
        maxSize = size;
        stackArray = new Object[maxSize];
        top = -1;
    }

    // push element onto the stack
    public void push(Object value) {
        if (top >= maxSize) {
            System.out.println("Stack is full. Cannot add " + value);
            return;
        }
        stackArray[++top] = value;
    }

    // pop element from the stack
    public Object pop() {
        if (top < 0) {
            System.out.println("Stack is empty.");
            return null;
        }
        return stackArray[top--];
    }

    // peek top element of the stack
    public Object peek() {
        if (top < 0) {
            System.out.println("Stack is empty.");
            return null;
        }
        return stackArray[top];
    }
}
```

You can use this class in the following way:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    Stack myStack = new Stack(5);
    myStack.push("First");
    myStack.push("Second");
    System.out.println(myStack.peek());  // prints "Second"
    System.out.println(myStack.pop());   // prints "Second"
    System.out.println(myStack.peek());  // prints "First"
}
```

Here is a simple implementation of a stack in Java using an array:

```java
public class Stack {
    private int maxSize;
    private long[] stackArray;
    private int top;

    // constructor
    public Stack(int size) {
        maxSize = size;
        stackArray = new long[maxSize];
        top = -1;
    }

    // push element onto the stack
    public void push(long value) {
        if (top >= maxSize - 1) {
            System.out.println("Stack is full. Can't add " + value);
            return;
        }
        stackArray[++top] = value;
    }

    // pop element from the stack
    public long pop() {
        if (top < 0) {
            System.out.println("Stack is empty.");
            return -1;
        }
        return stackArray[top--];
    }

    // peek at the top element of the stack
    public long peek() {
        if (top < 0) {
            System.out.println("Stack is empty.");
            return -1;
        }
        return stackArray[top];
    }

    // check if the stack is empty
    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return (top == -1);
    }

    // check if the stack is full
    public boolean isFull() {
        return (top == maxSize - 1);
    }
}
```
This Stack class has a constructor that takes an integer argument to specify the maximum size of the stack. The `push` method adds elements onto the top of the stack, and the `pop` method removes them from the top of the stack. The `peek` method allows you to view the element at the top of the stack without removing it. The methods `isEmpty` and `isFull` are used to check if the stack is empty or full respectively.

