Here is a Java implementation of the Bisection Method for finding roots of an equation. The Bisection method is a root-finding algorithm that applies binary search.

```java
public class BisectionMethod {

  private static final double ACCURACY = 0.01; // define accuracy level

  // function definition
  static double f(double x) {
    return (x * x * x) - (2 * x) - 5;
  }

  public static void main(String[] args) {
    double a = -200.0, b = 300.0; // define initial interval [a,b] such that f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs

    if (f(a) * f(b) >= 0) {
      System.out.println("Invalid interval");
      return;
    }

    double c = a; // define initial approximation

    while ((b - a) >= ACCURACY) {
      c = (a + b) / 2; // compute mid-point

      // check if middle point is root
      if (f(c) == 0.0)
        break;

      else if (f(c) * f(a) < 0)
        b = c; // update the interval to [a, c]
      else
        a = c; // update the interval to [c, b]
    }

    System.out.printf("The value of root is : %.4f\n", c);
  }
}
```
This program finds a root for the function `x^3 - 2x -5 = 0` on the interval [-200.0, 300.0] with an accuracy level of 0.01. If f(a) and f(b) have the same sign then it prints "Invalid Interval" and terminates.

The Bisection Method repeatedly bisects the interval [a, b] until the value at the midpoint c is within the desired accuracy level or the interval width becomes less than the accuracy level. If a root lies between `a` and `b` then the method will find it.

