Here is the Java code for implementing a simple version of the QR algorithm to find eigenvalues and eigenvectors:

```java
public class QREigen {

  private static final double EPS = 1.0e-9;

  // Implements the Householder transformation.
  static void householder(double[] a, int i) {
    int n = a.length;
    double scale = 0.0;
    for (int j = i; j < n; ++j)
      scale += Math.abs(a[j]);
    if (Math.abs(scale) < EPS) return;
    double h = 0.0, c, s;
    for (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {
      h += a[j]*a[j];
      a[j] /= scale;
    }
    double alpha = Math.sqrt(h);
    if (a[i] > 0) alpha = -alpha;
    h -= a[i]*alpha*scale;
    c = h/scale; s = scale;
    for (int j = i+1; j < n; ++j) {
      double temp = c*a[j] + s*a[j-1];
      a[j-1] =  s*a[j] - c*a[j-1];
      a[j]   = temp;
    }
    a[n-1] *= scale/alpha; a[i] -= alpha;
  }

  // Implements the QR algorithm.
  static void qr(double[] a, double[][] z) {
    int n = a.length;
    for (int i = 0; i < n-1; ++i) {
      householder(a, i+1);
      for (int j = i+1; j < n; ++j) {
        double c = a[j], s = z[j][i];
        for (int k = 0; k <= i; ++k) {
          double temp    = c*z[k][i] + s*z[k][j];
          z[k][j]   =  s*z[k][i] - c*z[k][j];
          z[k][i]   = temp;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  public static void main(String[] args) {
    double[][] a = {{5, 4, 1}, {3, -2, 1}, {6, 7, 8}};
    int n = a.length;
    double[] b = new double[n];
    double[][] z = new double[n][n];

    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
      b[i] = a[i][i];
      z[i][i] = 1.0;
    }

    qr(b, z);

    System.out.println("Eigenvalues:");
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) System.out.format("%8.4f\n", b[i]);

    System.out.println("\nEigenvectors:");
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
      for (int j = 0; j <= i; ++j) System.out.format("%8.4f ", z[j][i]);
      System.out.println();
    }
  }
}
```
This code finds the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of a real symmetric matrix using the QR algorithm. The `householder` function computes the Householder transformation for the given column of the matrix. The `qr` function applies this transformation to all columns, effectively performing the QR decomposition of the matrix. The main method demonstrates how to use these functions by finding the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of a 3x3 symmetric matrix.

