Here is a simple implementation of a stack in Java using an array. This stack can hold any type of objects, but for simplicity's sake let's use integers:

```java
public class Stack {
    private int maxSize; 
    private int top;  
    private Integer[] stackArray;   

    // constructor 
    public Stack(int size) { 
        maxSize = size; 
        top = -1; 
        stackArray = new Integer[maxSize]; 
    } 

    // push element onto the stack
    public void push(Integer value) { 
        if (top >= maxSize - 1) {  
            System.out.println("Stack is full, cannot add " + value); 
            return; 
        } 
        stackArray[++top] = value; 
    } 

    // pop element from the stack
    public Integer pop() { 
        if (top < 0) {  
            System.out.println("Stack is empty, cannot remove any item"); 
            return null; 
        } 
        return stackArray[top--]; 
    } 

    // check if the stack is empty
    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return (top == -1);
    } 

    // check if the stack is full
    public boolean isFull() {
        return (top == maxSize - 1);
    }  
}
```
This class can be used in this way:

```java
Stack myStack = new Stack(5); // create a stack of size 5
myStack.push(10); // add the number 10 to the stack
Integer poppedItem = myStack.pop(); // remove an item from the stack and store it in poppedItem
System.out.println("Popped item: " + poppedItem); // prints "Popped item: 10"
```

The `createNumber` method is a utility method in Apache Commons Lang library that can parse a string into a Number object. It supports several number types like Integer, Long, Float, Double and BigInteger etc. The method takes care of the type conversions based on the format of the input string.

