Here is the implementation of the Bisection Method in Java. This method finds a root of a real-valued function on an interval [a, b] where f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs.

```java
public class BisectionMethod {
    private static final double TOLERANCE = 0.0001; // tolerance level for approximation

    // Function that receives a value x and return the evaluation of the function f(x)
    private static double f(double x) {
        return Math.cos(x) - x;  // example function
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        bisection(-1, 2); // initial interval [a, b] where f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs
    }

    private static void bisection(double a, double b) {
        if (f(a) * f(b) >= 0) {
            System.out.println("The function has the same sign at interval endpoints. Bisection method fails.");
            return;
        }

        double c = a;  // initial approximation for root

        while ((b - a) >= TOLERANCE) {
            c = (a + b) / 2; // new midpoint

            if (f(c) == 0.0) {
                break; // if f(midpoint) is zero, then the midpoint is the root
            } else if (f(a) * f(c) < 0) {  // if f(left endpoint) and f(midpoint) have opposite signs
                b = c; // update right endpoint to the midpoint
            } else {
                a = c; // otherwise, update left endpoint to the midpoint
            }
        }

        System.out.printf("The root is: %.4f\n", c);
    }
}
```
This program uses a function `f` that represents an example function (cosine minus x). The `bisection` method checks if the signs of the function evaluated at the interval endpoints are opposite; if not, it prints an error message and returns. If they have opposite signs, it enters a loop where it calculates midpoints and updates one of the endpoints until the difference between the right endpoint and left endpoint is less than the tolerance level. At each iteration, it checks whether the function evaluated at the midpoint is zero (in which case the midpoint is the root) or has a different sign from the function evaluated at the left endpoint (in which case it updates the right endpoint to be the midpoint). If the function evaluated at the midpoint and the function evaluated at the left endpoint have the same sign, it updates the left endpoint to be the midpoint. Finally, if the difference between the right endpoint and left endpoint is less than the tolerance level, it prints the root (which is the midpoint of the last iteration).

The code you posted is a part of Apache Commons Math library, specifically the `BisectionSolver` class. It's a root-finding algorithm that uses bisection method to find roots in a given interval [x0, x1] where f(x0) and f(x1) have opposite signs.

