Here's an example of how to minimize exits in a block of code using JavaScript:

```javascript
function minimizeExits(block) {
    let ifNodes = Array.from(block.querySelectorAll('if'));
    for (let iNode of ifNodes) {
        let trueBlock = iNode.getElementsByTagName('true')[0];
        let falseBlock = iNode.getElementsByTagName('false')[0] || document.createElement('block');
        minimizeExits(trueBlock);
        minimizeExits(falseBlock);
        if (trueBlock.childElementCount === 1 && trueBlock.lastChild.tagName === 'BREAK' && falseBlock.childElementCount > 0) {
            let newIf = document.createElement('if');
            newIf.appendChild(document.createElement('condition'));
            newIf.firstChild.appendChild(trueNode.cloneNode());
            newIf.appendChild(falseBlock.cloneNode(true));
            iNode.parentElement.insertBefore(newIf, iNode);
            while (iNode.childElementCount > 0) {
                let child = iNode.lastChild;
                iNode.removeChild(child);
                ifNodes.push(minimizeExits(child));
            }
        }
    }
    return block;
}
```
This function takes a block of HTML code and recursively minimizes the number of exits in if-else statements by converting them into nested if-statements. It uses querySelectorAll to find all 'if' nodes, then it gets the trueBlock and falseBlock for each if node and calls minimizeExits on these blocks. If there is only one child in the true block which is a break statement and the false block has more than 0 children, it creates a new if-statement with the condition negated and the falseBlock as the trueBlock of this new if-statement. It then inserts this new if-statement before the original if node and removes all nodes from the original if node and pushes them to the ifNodes array for further processing.

Please note that this is a JavaScript code example, but it's not directly executable because the environment doesn't support HTML elements and methods used in the code. You can use this logic to write a function in your respective language or framework which supports DOM manipulation.

