The `createNumber` method in Apache Commons Lang's NumberUtils class is used to convert a String into a Number object. It supports several number types including Integer, Long, Float, Double and BigInteger.

Here is an example of how you can use it:

```java
import org.apache.commons.lang3.math.NumberUtils;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Convert a String to a Number object
        String numberStr = "1234567890";
        Number number = NumberUtils.createNumber(numberStr);
        System.out.println(number.getClass() + ": " + number);
    }
}
```
In this example, the `createNumber` method will try to convert the string into an Integer first. If it can't be converted to an Integer (because the number is too big), then it tries a Long, and so on until it finds the biggest type that can hold the number. 

If you want to specify the return type of `createNumber` method, you should use methods like `createInteger(String)`, `createLong(String)`, etc. For example:

