Here is a simple implementation of a stack in Java using an array:

```java
public class Stack {
    private int maxSize;
    private long[] stackArray;
    private int top;

    // constructor
    public Stack(int size) {
        maxSize = size;
        stackArray = new long[maxSize];
        top = -1;
    }

    // push an element onto the stack
    public void push(long value) {
        if (top >= maxSize - 1) {
            System.out.println("Stack is full. Can't add " + value);
            return;
        }
        stackArray[++top] = value;
    }

    // pop an element from the stack
    public long pop() {
        if (top < 0) {
            System.out.println("Stack is empty.");
            return -1;
        }
        return stackArray[top--];
    }

    // peek at the top element of the stack
    public long peek() {
        if (top < 0) {
            System.out.println("Stack is empty.");
            return -1;
        }
        return stackArray[top];
    }

    // check if the stack is empty
    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return (top == -1);
    }

    // check if the stack is full
    public boolean isFull() {
        return (top == maxSize - 1);
    }
}
```
This class represents a simple stack data structure. The `push` method adds an element to the top of the stack, the `pop` method removes and returns the top element from the stack, and the `peek` method simply returns the top element without removing it. The `isEmpty` and `isFull` methods are used to check if the stack is empty or full respectively.

