Here is a Java code snippet that uses the Bisection Method to find roots of an equation. The function `computeObjectiveValue(double x)` should be replaced with your actual function.

```java
public class BisectionMethod {
    private static final double TOLERANCE = 0.0001; // Adjust this according to the precision you need

    // Replace this method body with your own equation
    private static double computeObjectiveValue(double x) {
        return Math.cos(x) - x;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double a = 0.0, b = 1.0; // Initial interval [a,b] where the root is expected

        if (computeObjectiveValue(a) * computeObjectiveValue(b) >= 0) {
            System.out.println("The initial values do not bracket the root.");
            return;
        }

        double c = a; // Initialize result

        while ((b - a) >= TOLERANCE) {
            // Find middle point
            c = (a + b) / 2;

            // Check if middle point is root
            if (computeObjectiveValue(c) == 0.0 || Math.abs(computeObjectiveValue(c)) < TOLERANCE)
                break;

              // Decide the next interval
            else if (computeObjectiveValue(c) * computeObjectiveValue(a) < 0)
                b = c;
            else
                a = c;
        }

        System.out.println("The value of root is : " + c);
    }
}
```
This code will find the root of the equation `cos(x) - x` in the interval [0,1] with a precision of 0.0001. If you want to use this for another function or an other interval, replace the `computeObjectiveValue()` method accordingly and adjust the initial interval as needed.

