Here is the implementation of the Bisection Method in Java. This method finds a root of a real-valued function on an interval [a, b] where f(a) and f(b) have different signs.

```java
public class BisectionMethod {
    private static final double TOLERANCE = 0.0001; // tolerance level for approximation

    // Function that receives a value x and returns the evaluation of the function f(x)
    private static double function(double x) {
        return Math.cos(x) - x;  // example function
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        bisection(-1, 2); // initial interval [a, b] where f(a) and f(b) have different signs
    }

    private static void bisection(double a, double b) {
        if (function(a) * function(b) >= 0) {
            System.out.println("Invalid interval [" + a + ", " + b + "] for the given function.");
            return;
        }

        double c = a;  // initial approximation of root

        while ((b - a) >= TOLERANCE) {
            c = (a + b) / 2; // new midpoint

            if (function(c) == 0.0 || Math.abs(b - a) < TOLERANCE) {  // if c is the root or close enough to it
                break;
            } else if (function(a) * function(c) < 0) { // if f(a) and f(c) have different signs, then root lies in [a, c]
                b = c;
            } else {  // otherwise the root lies in [c, b]
                a = c;
            }
        }

        System.out.printf("The value of root is : %.4f\n" ,c);
    }
}
```
This program uses a function `function()` to evaluate the value of a mathematical function at point x. The main method calls the bisection() method with an initial interval [a, b] where f(a) and f(b) have different signs. If this is not the case, it prints an error message and returns from the method.

The bisection() method then enters a loop that continues until the difference between the endpoints of the interval becomes smaller than a predefined tolerance level `TOLERANCE`. In each iteration of the loop, it calculates the midpoint c of the current interval [a, b], and checks if f(c) is zero (or close to it due to rounding errors) or if the difference between b and a is smaller than TOLERANCE. If either condition holds true, the method breaks out from the loop because c is now an approximation of the root of the function within the specified tolerance level.

If f(a) and f(c) have different signs, it means that the root lies in [a, c] and thus updates b to be c; otherwise, the root lies in [c, b] so it updates a to be c. This process is repeated until the difference between b and a becomes smaller than TOLERANCE or f(c) becomes zero (or close to it).

