Here is a simple implementation of a Number to Words converter in Java. This program converts numbers upto 999,999,999,999,999:

```java
public class NumberToWords {
    private static final String[] units = { "", "One", "Two", "Three", "Four", "Five", "Six", "Seven", "Eight", "Nine", 
        "Ten", "Eleven", "Twelve", "Thirteen", "Fourteen", "Fifteen", "Sixteen", "Seventeen", "Eighteen", "Nineteen" };
    private static final String[] tens = { "", "", "Twenty", "Thirty", "Forty", "Fifty", "Sixty", "Seventy", "Eighty", 
        "Ninety" };

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(convert(123456789)); // One Hundred Twenty Three Million Four Hundred Fifty Six Thousand Seven Hundred Eighty Nine
    }

    private static String convert(final int n) {
        if (n < 0) {
            return "Minus " + convert(-n);
        }
        if (n < 20) {
            return units[n];
        }
        if (n < 100) {
            return tens[n / 10] + ((n % 10 > 0) ? " " + convert(n % 10) : "");
        }
        if (n < 1000) {
            return units[n / 100] + " Hundred" + ((n % 100 > 0) ? " " + convert(n % 100) : "");
        }
        if (n < 1000000) {
            return convert(n / 1000) + " Thousand" + ((n % 1000 > 0) ? " " + convert(n % 1000) : "");
        }
        if (n < 1000000000) {
            return convert(n / 1000000) + " Million" + ((n % 1000000 > 0) ? " " + convert(n % 1000000) : "");
        }
        if (n < 1000000000000L) {
            return convert(n / 1000000000) + " Billion" + ((n % 1000000000 > 0) ? " " + convert(n % 1000000000) : "");
        }
        if (n < 1000000000000000L) {
            return convert(n / 1000000000000L) + " Trillion" + ((n % 1000000000000L > 0) ? " " + convert(n % 1000000000000L) : "");
        }
        return convert(n / 1000000000000000L) + " Quadrillion" + ((n % 1000000000000000L > 0) ? " " + convert(n % 1000000000000000L) : "");
    }
}
```

